{
  "element_layout": {
    "score": 9,
    "feedback": "The six supplied frames show legible light text on a dark background, stable left alignment, and consistent category colors. The title, stage label, and explanatory sentence establish a basic hierarchy. However, the instructional content occupies a small portion of each frame, leaving substantial unused space. No distinct presentation-and-animation arrangement or visible graphical teaching area is apparent. The statement that bar lengths represent exponential masses is unsupported by any visible bars in these samples. The shared total and final probability labels are spatially separated without connectors or a visible common container. Larger mathematical content and an integrated quantitative diagram would make the layout more useful for learning."
  },
  "attractiveness": {
    "score": 6,
    "feedback": "The restrained dark palette and cyan, purple, and orange category coding are harmonious and provide recognizable identities. Nevertheless, the sampled presentation is almost entirely text, with little visual variation or illustration. The exponential stage appears essentially unchanged between 4.0 and 6.5 seconds, though intervening motion cannot be assessed. No visible mass bars, combining operation, or probability partition provides a compelling visual representation of the concept. The result is orderly but visually sparse. Animation quality, effects, and sustained engagement cannot be established from still frames alone."
  },
  "logic_flow": {
    "score": 14,
    "feedback": "The sampled sequence follows an appropriate instructional order: signed logits at 1.0 seconds, exponentials at 4.0 and 6.5 seconds, a shared total at 8.0 seconds, division by that total at 11.0 seconds, and normalized probabilities at 17.5 seconds. This scaffolding makes the numerical procedure traceable. Consistent category colors help connect inputs and outputs. However, stages mostly replace one another, limiting direct comparison, and the total is presented without a visible addition expression connecting its three contributors. English stage labels within Chinese instructional text may also reduce accessibility for some learners. The timestamps establish sequence, but transition smoothness, narration synchronization, and actual pacing remain unverified."
  },
  "accuracy_depth": {
    "score": 13,
    "feedback": "The displayed calculations are correct to the stated precision: exp(-1) ≈ 0.368, exp(0.7) ≈ 2.014, and exp(1.3) ≈ 3.669; their unrounded sum rounds to 6.051. The normalized probabilities round to 0.061, 0.333, and 0.606 and sum to 1.000 at the displayed precision. The negative input is a useful example of exponentiation producing a positive value. However, the supplied frames do not explicitly explain that every exponential is positive, show the general softmax formula, or explain why dividing every mass by the same sum produces probabilities totaling one. The repeated claim about proportional bar lengths and the final claim about filling a shared total are not demonstrated graphically in the visible evidence. The example teaches calculation more effectively than the intended mass-and-shared-total interpretation."
  },
  "visual_consistency": {
    "score": 16,
    "feedback": "Background, title placement, text styling, and category colors remain consistent across the six samples. Cyan consistently identifies category 1, purple category 2, and orange category 3, providing continuity through the numerical transformations. The shared total uses neutral text appropriately. The final stage changes vertical spacing and places the concluding statement farther toward the center, slightly weakening spatial continuity. Mixed Chinese and English labels and inconsistent spacing around some mathematical expressions could be standardized. These frames support a judgment of strong static consistency, but they cannot establish coherent motion timing or smooth integration of animated elements."
  },
  "overall_score": 58,
  "summary": "Based only on six timestamped still frames, this presentation offers a correct, logically ordered numerical softmax example but only partially teaches the intended visual concept. Signed inputs become positive exponentials and then probabilities through a shared denominator, yet proportional masses and the common unit total are stated rather than visibly demonstrated. No full video or audio was supplied, so motion quality, transitions, narration, synchronization, and continuous pacing cannot be evaluated. The teaching approach is suitable as a worked calculation; adding quantitative graphics and explicit conceptual explanations would make it substantially more effective.",
  "strengths": [
    "Correct exponential values, shared denominator, and normalized probabilities at the displayed precision.",
    "Clear progression from signed inputs to exponentiation, summation, division, and final probabilities.",
    "Stable category colors help learners trace corresponding values across stages.",
    "The negative input concretely illustrates that an exponential output can be positive despite a negative logit.",
    "Readable contrast and consistent static styling across the sampled frames."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Show signed inputs around a zero baseline, then transform them into positive exponential bars using a common linear scale.",
    "Display the sum explicitly: Z = exp(-1) + exp(0.7) + exp(1.3) ≈ 6.051, with visual connections to all three masses.",
    "Scale all three masses by the same denominator and assemble the resulting colored segments into a visible unit-total bar.",
    "Add the general formula p_i = exp(z_i) / Σ_j exp(z_j), explaining positivity and why the probabilities sum to one.",
    "Keep input, exponential mass, and probability values available for comparison through aligned rows or persistent labels.",
    "Increase the size and spatial prominence of the main teaching graphics, and standardize stage-label language and notation.",
    "Review the full video and audio separately to assess motion, pacing, transitions, and narration synchronization."
  ]
}
